course: dictionary
lesson: 15333
title: Snooker and its many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A word can have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one the writer means. «Sam chalked his cue before his shot on the snooker table.» Here snooker is a game on a green table. The words cue, chalk and table are your clues.
explain: Now read «Ravi could not take his shot, because the white ball was in a snooker behind a red.» Here a snooker is a position in the game, where one ball blocks the shot you need. Ask: what would fit in the sentence? A tricky spot in the game fits.
explain: Snooker can also be a verb. «The clever fox snookered the hunters by taking the stream.» Here it means to trick someone or leave them with no way out. Try "tricked" in the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
explain: In everyday talk, «I'm snookered, I forgot my homework and the bus has gone» means you are stuck with no way out. Swap in "I'm stuck" and the sentence still makes sense, so you have the right meaning.
fact: Snooker is a game played on a large table covered in green cloth.
fact: Players hit coloured balls into pockets with a long stick called a cue.
fact: Red balls are worth 1 point each, and the coloured balls are worth 2 to 7 points.
fact: «Snookered» means stuck, beaten or trapped, especially in everyday talk.
fact: Snooker is a popular sport on television in the UK, Ireland and Australia.
exercise: «Mia chalked her cue and lined up a shot on the snooker table.» What is a snooker table? | A wooden box for storing balls | *A large table covered in green cloth for playing snooker | A table for eating dinner at | A small table in a classroom
exercise: «Dad's shot was blocked, so he was left in a snooker.» What does snooker mean here? | A kind of hot drink | A loud noise | A type of dog | *A position where a ball blocks the shot you need
exercise: «I'm snookered because I left my wallet at home.» What does snookered mean? | Very rich | *Stuck, with no way out | Lost in a park | Happy and full of energy
test: «The cue is the long stick players use to hit the balls.» In snooker, what is a cue? | A pocket for the balls | A green cloth on the table | *A long stick used to hit the balls | A kind of score
test: Sam says, «I'm snookered by this maths problem.» What does Sam mean? | *He is stuck and cannot find a way out | He is hungry for lunch | He has solved it very quickly | He is winning the maths game
exam: Which sentence uses snooker as a noun for the game? | They snookered the dog into the garden | *The children played snooker after school | Ella snookered her brother with a clever trick | The fox was snookered by the hunters
